{"title": "AK model", "kind": "Concept", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Advanced", "article": "AK model", "definition": {"text": "The simplest endogenous growth model, in which output is a linear function of capital, Y = AK.", "sent_ids": ["7de05d8966c5", "7306fdc220c9"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Its key feature is that capital has no diminishing returns, so growth need not fade to zero as in older models.", "sent_ids": ["7a9a7c0bcb71", "4956956fba15"]}, {"text": "It is a Cobb–Douglas function where the output elasticity of capital equals 1.", "sent_ids": ["904fe02904aa"]}, {"text": "Here K covers both physical and human capital, and A is a constant level of technology.", "sent_ids": ["df56d16595d2", "ad7e861efbc8"]}, {"text": "Textbooks mostly use it to introduce endogenous growth theory.", "sent_ids": ["e3343fa2ecf9"]}], "prerequisites": ["nature-econ-551", "nature-eecon-225"], "related": ["nature-eecon-204"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Ecological economics and sustainability", "chapter": "Growth theory and the environment", "id": "nature-eecon-200", "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article"], "sources": [{"title": "AK model", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AK_model", "revid": 1376835974, "sent_ids": ["7a9a7c0bcb71", "4956956fba15", "904fe02904aa", "df56d16595d2", "ad7e861efbc8", "e3343fa2ecf9", "7de05d8966c5", "7306fdc220c9"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": "Q294077", "score": 3.39, "score_parts": {"importance": 1.386, "weight": 0, "tests": 1, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true}
{"title": "Golden Rule savings rate", "kind": "Concept", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Advanced", "article": "Golden Rule savings rate", "definition": {"text": "The share of income saved that gives the highest level of consumption per person that can be kept up forever, as in the Solow–Swan model.", "sent_ids": ["3e576c10344e", "769222c6521a"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Saving 100% leaves nothing to consume, and saving 0% lets capital wear out, so the best rate lies in between.", "sent_ids": ["8adb72e84dfb", "fae214f9e561"]}, {"text": "Edmund Phelps named it in 1961, applying 'do unto others' between generations.", "sent_ids": ["133d9b3389bb"]}, {"text": "It is reached where the marginal product of capital equals population growth plus depreciation.", "sent_ids": ["3220dc456407"]}, {"text": "An economy saving above this rate is dynamically inefficient: it could consume more both now and later by saving less.", "sent_ids": ["e115a0a36482", "2d18aa435ac5"]}], "prerequisites": ["nature-eecon-204"], "related": ["nature-eecon-224", "nature-eecon-228"], "events": [{"date_text": "1961", "sent_ids": ["133d9b3389bb"]}], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Ecological economics and sustainability", "chapter": "Growth theory and the environment", "id": "nature-eecon-201", "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article", "mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "Golden Rule savings rate", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_Rule_savings_rate", "revid": 1384849427, "sent_ids": ["8adb72e84dfb", "fae214f9e561", "133d9b3389bb", "3220dc456407", "3e576c10344e", "769222c6521a"]}, {"title": "Dynamic efficiency", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dynamic_efficiency", "revid": 1383108239, "sent_ids": ["e115a0a36482", "2d18aa435ac5"]}], "flags": [], "qid": "Q1535347", "score": 6.35, "score_parts": {"importance": 2.773, "weight": 0, "tests": 2, "pages": 1.58, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true}
{"title": "Population growth", "kind": "Process", "weight": "Major", "level": "Foundational", "article": "Population growth", "definition": {"text": "The increase in the number of people in a population.", "sent_ids": ["846ef6f2410a"]}, "facts": [{"text": "World population grew from 1 billion in 1800 to 8.3 billion in 2026, and now adds about 70 million people, or 0.84%, a year.", "sent_ids": ["240a532f9e3d", "d0845da2499f"]}, {"text": "The global growth rate peaked at 2.2% a year in 1963 and has fallen since.", "sent_ids": ["239e7f953396"]}, {"text": "Better farming, sanitation and medicine drove growth; the demographic transition has slowed it in rich countries.", "sent_ids": ["53c484788bf1", "d7dd940edac5"]}, {"text": "As of 2024 the UN projects a peak of about 10.3 billion in the mid-2080s.", "sent_ids": ["e11a9c8fecaf"]}, {"text": "Projections differ: the Institute for Health Metrics and Evaluation expects a peak of 9.73 billion in 2064, falling to 8.89 billion by 2100.", "sent_ids": ["0a0de224bb16"]}, {"text": "Together with rising consumption, it drives biodiversity loss and climate change through overuse of natural resources.", "sent_ids": ["b6350bfdc24c"]}, {"text": "Population reduction is discussed as a sustainability strategy, though its potential is limited by respect for free life choices.", "sent_ids": ["955d397539b9"]}], "prerequisites": [], "related": ["nature-eecon-203", "nature-clim-265", "nature-res-234"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": ["Logistic growth and carrying capacity"], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Ecological economics and sustainability", "chapter": "Growth theory and the environment", "id": "nature-eecon-202", "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article", "prerequisite of 2+"], "sources": [{"title": "Population growth", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Population_growth", "revid": 1381963414, "sent_ids": ["240a532f9e3d", "d0845da2499f", "239e7f953396", "53c484788bf1", "d7dd940edac5", "e11a9c8fecaf", "0a0de224bb16", "b6350bfdc24c", "955d397539b9", "846ef6f2410a"]}], "events": [{"date_text": "in 1800", "date_ma": 0.00015, "sent_ids": ["240a532f9e3d"]}, {"date_text": "in 1963", "date_ma": -1.3e-05, "sent_ids": ["239e7f953396"]}], "flags": [], "qid": "Q386191", "score": 20.35, "score_parts": {"importance": 8.349, "weight": 3, "tests": 2, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 6.0}, "selected": true}
{"title": "Logistic growth and carrying capacity", "kind": "Concept", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Intermediate", "article": "Population growth#Logistic equation", "definition": {"text": "A model in which a population's growth slows as it nears the carrying capacity, the largest population its environment can sustain.", "sent_ids": ["31234fe132d8", "1a6efad0a185"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Most non-human populations follow a logistic rather than exponential path.", "sent_ids": ["1820ae035878"]}, {"text": "At carrying capacity the curve levels off, usually when the population has used up most of its natural resources.", "sent_ids": ["b8f5094cba5b"]}, {"text": "Human population growth has followed a roughly linear trend over the last few decades.", "sent_ids": ["63fbf725bf6a"]}], "prerequisites": ["nature-eecon-202"], "related": ["nature-eco-593"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": ["Population growth"], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Ecological economics and sustainability", "chapter": "Growth theory and the environment", "id": "nature-eecon-203", "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own section"], "sources": [{"title": "Population growth", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Population_growth", "revid": 1381963414, "sent_ids": ["1820ae035878", "b8f5094cba5b", "63fbf725bf6a", "31234fe132d8", "1a6efad0a185"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": null, "score": 10.35, "score_parts": {"importance": 8.349, "weight": 0, "tests": 1, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true}
{"title": "Solow–Swan model", "kind": "Concept", "weight": "Landmark", "level": "Intermediate", "article": "Solow–Swan model", "definition": {"text": "An economic model that explains long-run growth through capital accumulation, labour or population growth, and rising productivity from technological progress.", "sent_ids": ["b54eaeadca84", "414228c56061"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Robert Solow and Trevor Swan developed it independently in 1956; Solow won the Nobel Prize in Economics in 1987 for this work.", "sent_ids": ["db194b52f6a4", "9bc2d1fee014"]}, {"text": "Its key assumption is diminishing returns to capital: each extra unit of capital adds less output than the one before.", "sent_ids": ["2f45756f67a1", "41df0832e70c"]}, {"text": "Without technological progress, growth stops once new investment only replaces worn-out capital.", "sent_ids": ["363799fbd800"]}, {"text": "In the long run, lasting growth in income per person comes only from technological progress; saving and population growth change only levels.", "sent_ids": ["5c9a2e3af411", "390718894997"]}, {"text": "It is called an exogenous growth model because it does not explain why technology improves.", "sent_ids": ["cb4852635862"]}, {"text": "It predicts poor countries should grow faster and catch up with rich ones, which is seldom seen in capital flows (Lucas' paradox).", "sent_ids": ["3c3193a158b3", "bd5b5b4b9465"]}, {"text": "It remains a common textbook model of long-run growth.", "sent_ids": ["270139d104f8"]}, {"text": "Output grows only through more capital, a larger population, or technology that raises total factor productivity.", "sent_ids": ["f002acb305c4"]}], "prerequisites": ["nature-econ-478", "nature-eecon-225", "nature-econ-539", "nature-econ-519"], "related": ["nature-econ-551", "nature-eecon-201", "nature-eecon-223", "nature-econ-599"], "events": [{"date_text": "1956", "sent_ids": ["db194b52f6a4"]}], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": ["Solow residual", "Mankiw–Romer–Weil model", "Conditional convergence", "Endogenous growth theory"], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "mention_sent_ids": ["bc13d9482a1f", "b2ad044b4227", "27c529664ed2", "7724cf8a9442", "6ce999bacb71"], "part": "Ecological economics and sustainability", "chapter": "Growth theory and the environment", "id": "nature-eecon-204", "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article", "mentioned on 2+ pages", "prerequisite of 2+"], "sources": [{"title": "Solow–Swan model", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solow%E2%80%93Swan_model", "revid": 1381324869, "sent_ids": ["db194b52f6a4", "9bc2d1fee014", "2f45756f67a1", "41df0832e70c", "363799fbd800", "5c9a2e3af411", "390718894997", "3c3193a158b3", "bd5b5b4b9465", "b54eaeadca84", "414228c56061"]}, {"title": "Macroeconomics", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Macroeconomics", "revid": 1385318418, "sent_ids": ["270139d104f8", "f002acb305c4", "bc13d9482a1f", "b2ad044b4227", "27c529664ed2", "7724cf8a9442", "6ce999bacb71"]}, {"title": "Economic growth", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Economic_growth", "revid": 1386224088, "sent_ids": ["cb4852635862"]}], "flags": [], "qid": "Q777494", "score": 23.11, "score_parts": {"importance": 4.605, "weight": 6, "tests": 3, "pages": 2.0, "builds_on_it": 7.5}, "selected": true}
{"title": "Solow residual", "kind": "Concept", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Advanced", "article": "Solow–Swan model#Variations in the effects of productivity", "definition": {"text": "The part of output growth left unexplained after accounting for capital accumulation, taken as a measure of total factor productivity growth.", "sent_ids": ["fd7a21034acd", "620b9382c688"]}, "facts": [{"text": "It is often credited entirely to technological progress but also includes lasting gains in how efficiently inputs are combined.", "sent_ids": ["ad93505ab38e"]}, {"text": "Economists use Solow's growth accounting to separate the effects of technology, capital and labour on growth.", "sent_ids": ["d6a5db26a194"]}], "prerequisites": ["nature-eecon-204"], "related": ["nature-econ-551"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": ["Solow–Swan model", "Mankiw–Romer–Weil model", "Conditional convergence"], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Ecological economics and sustainability", "chapter": "Growth theory and the environment", "id": "nature-eecon-205", "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own section"], "sources": [{"title": "Solow–Swan model", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solow%E2%80%93Swan_model", "revid": 1381324869, "sent_ids": ["ad93505ab38e", "d6a5db26a194", "fd7a21034acd", "620b9382c688"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": null, "score": 6.61, "score_parts": {"importance": 4.605, "weight": 0, "tests": 1, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true}
{"title": "Mankiw–Romer–Weil model", "kind": "Concept", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Advanced", "article": "Solow–Swan model#Addition of human capital", "definition": {"text": "A 1992 version of the Solow–Swan model that adds human capital, such as education, as a factor of production.", "sent_ids": ["a54959641171"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Poor countries have lower output and returns to capital because they have less human capital.", "sent_ids": ["b4f2133ed830"]}, {"text": "The estimated model explained 78% of the variation in income across countries.", "sent_ids": ["b24c00543fdf"]}, {"text": "Adding human capital lowers the estimate of the total factor productivity residual.", "sent_ids": ["d00a5886fed4"]}], "prerequisites": ["nature-eecon-204", "nature-econ-531"], "related": ["nature-eecon-207"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": ["Solow–Swan model", "Solow residual", "Conditional convergence"], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Ecological economics and sustainability", "chapter": "Growth theory and the environment", "id": "nature-eecon-206", "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own section"], "sources": [{"title": "Solow–Swan model", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solow%E2%80%93Swan_model", "revid": 1381324869, "sent_ids": ["b4f2133ed830", "b24c00543fdf", "d00a5886fed4", "a54959641171"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": null, "score": 6.61, "score_parts": {"importance": 4.605, "weight": 0, "tests": 1, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true}
{"title": "Conditional convergence", "kind": "Concept", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Advanced", "article": "Solow–Swan model#Conditional convergence", "definition": {"text": "The prediction that poor countries' incomes catch up with rich countries' if they save similar shares of output in physical and human capital.", "sent_ids": ["3dad7ec684f6"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Since the 1950s output per worker in rich and poor countries has generally not converged, but poor countries that raised their saving rates have converged.", "sent_ids": ["c3431a84464a"]}, {"text": "Japan converged with rich countries after raising its saving rates in the 1950s and 1960s.", "sent_ids": ["df79517eb9f5", "4e257fb3f2bb"]}, {"text": "Endogenous growth theories are criticized for failing to explain the conditional convergence seen in the data.", "sent_ids": ["2f207eec3cad"]}], "prerequisites": ["nature-eecon-204"], "related": ["nature-econ-551", "nature-eecon-206"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": ["Solow–Swan model", "Solow residual", "Mankiw–Romer–Weil model"], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Ecological economics and sustainability", "chapter": "Growth theory and the environment", "id": "nature-eecon-207", "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own section", "mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "Solow–Swan model", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solow%E2%80%93Swan_model", "revid": 1381324869, "sent_ids": ["c3431a84464a", "df79517eb9f5", "4e257fb3f2bb", "3dad7ec684f6"]}, {"title": "Endogenous growth theory", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Endogenous_growth_theory", "revid": 1377648848, "sent_ids": ["2f207eec3cad"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": null, "score": 8.19, "score_parts": {"importance": 4.605, "weight": 0, "tests": 2, "pages": 1.58, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true}
